Fix a double free when a transcode chain fails to start.

Crash reproduced by streaming a VP9/VAAPI profile on hardware whose VAAPI has
no VP9 encode entrypoint: SIGSEGV inside libc's allocator, faulting address 0,
with a garbage pointer in rdi.  Backtrace (debug build, core from a DS918+):

    #0  0x00007f94fc86c0be in libc.so.6
    #2  profile_chain_close ()       at src/profile.c:2720
    #3  profile_transcode_work ()    at src/profile.c:2732
    #4  profile_chain_close ()       at src/profile.c:1160
    #5  profile_transcode_open ()    at src/profile.c:2805
    #6  http_stream_channel ()       at src/webui/webui.c:1538

profile_chain_close() appears twice on the same prch.  That is by design in
this path:

    profile_transcode_work():
        dst = prsh->prsh_transcoder = transcoder_create(...);
        if (!dst)
            goto fail;              /* VP9/VAAPI dies here */
        ...
        if (profile_sharer_postinit(prsh))   /* never reached */
            goto fail;
        return 0;
    fail:
        profile_chain_close(prch);           /* first close  */
        return -1;

    profile_transcode_open():
        r = profile_transcode_work(prch, ...);
        if (r) {
            profile_chain_close(prch);       /* second close */
            return r;
        }

profile_chain_close() is written to be idempotent -- prch_timeshift, prch_gh,
prch_tsfix, prch_muxer, prch_st, prch_sq_used, prch_linked, prch_pro and
prch_id are all NULLed after being released.  prch_sharer is the one that is
not: profile_sharer_destroy() clears it in the "other chains remain" branch
(both arms), but in the "last chain, destroy the sharer" branch it is cleared
only inside

    if (prsh->prsh_queue_run) { ... prch->prch_sharer = NULL; ... }

and prsh_queue_run is set by profile_sharer_postinit(), which the failure path
above never reaches.  So the first close does LIST_REMOVE(), finds the chain
list empty, skips the queue_run block, and free()s prsh while prch->prch_sharer
still points at it.  The second close reads that dangling pointer, passes the
"if (prsh == NULL) return" guard, runs LIST_REMOVE() through freed memory and
free()s prsh again -- heap corruption, then the crash in libc.

Clearing the two pointers before free(prsh) makes the second close the no-op
it was meant to be.

Confirmed on the reporting hardware: the same VP9/VAAPI request that produced
the core now fails cleanly, and the teardown completes with no crash.

    transcode: 0007: failed to find codec profile: 'webtv-vp9-vaapi'
    http: 192.168.80.88: HTTP/1.1 GET (1) /stream/channel -- 503
    subscription: 0069: "HTTP" unsubscribing from "TVA", client="VLC/3.0.23"
    mpegts: ... - close PID 0030 (48) ... - stopping mux

Note the failure is total, not a degradation: the codec profile is missing, so
transcoder_create() dies before any elementary stream is set up and the client
gets a 503 rather than an audio-only stream. That is the pre-existing upstream
policy for a missing codec profile, not something this patch changes.  The assignment inside the queue_run block is left alone:
it happens under prsh_queue_mutex and before pthread_join(), where the ordering
matters.

NOT introduced by PR #2117.  Both the double close and profile_sharer_destroy()
are identical in master (45cbe4adb); the PR's only changes to profile.c are in
profile_sharer_deliver(), profile_class_mc_list() and profile_transcode_mc_valid().
The PR merely makes the bug reachable by shipping hardware profiles that can
fail at transcoder_create() on devices lacking the entrypoint.  Worth sending
upstream on its own.

--- src/profile.c.orig
+++ src/profile.c
@@ -998,6 +998,12 @@ profile_sharer_destroy(profile_chain_t *prch)
 #endif
     if (prsh->prsh_start_msg)
       streaming_start_unref(prsh->prsh_start_msg);
+    /* prsh is about to be freed, so no chain may keep a pointer to it.  Only
+       the prsh_queue_run path above cleared these, which leaves prch_sharer
+       dangling for a chain torn down before the queue thread ever started --
+       and profile_chain_close() is called twice on exactly that path. */
+    prch->prch_sharer = NULL;
+    prch->prch_post_share = NULL;
     free(prsh);
   } else {
     if (prsh->prsh_queue_run) {
